1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,780
Module 1, Video 1.3: The Once-Only Promise

2
00:00:04,580 --> 00:00:05,740
Yeah, once only,

3
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:11,340
which, um, just means the state never asks a citizen for the same data twice.

4
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:18,000
That is the outcome interoperability is for, and really the ultimate test of whether it actually works.

5
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:24,760
Right. Because, I mean, it is so easy to get lost in the technical requirements of the reference architecture, you know, all the buses and the standards.

6
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:32,580
But when you are managing these systems, what is the actual outcome of this framework for the citizen interacting with your ministry?

7
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:36,600
Well, the outcome is simply that the state never asks for the same information twice.

8
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:37,260
Just once.

9
00:00:37,960 --> 00:00:38,380
Exactly.

10
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:41,260
A citizen provides her identity once,

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,540
and from that point forward, every service entitled to that data can just retrieve it.

12
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:48,860
Assuming they have a lawful basis, right?

13
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:52,940
Yes. Provided there is a lawful basis and, uh, her consent.

14
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:55,680
They can get it without asking her to provide it again.

15
00:00:56,620 --> 00:01:01,000
That single capability is really the clearest test of whether your framework is functioning.

16
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:05,120
So let's consider the operational reality of siloed systems.

17
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:09,880
Um, what happens in your ministry when agencies lack this once only capability?

18
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:17,100
Well, when systems remain siloed, your agencies just ask the same citizen for the duplicated proof repeatedly.

19
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:19,780
Like, the identity office checks a document.

20
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:23,820
Right. Then the school checks that same paper document again at enrollment.

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,260
Oh, wow. And then the clinic checks it a third time.

22
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:29,880
Because siloed agencies cannot query each other directly,

23
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:30,820
um,

24
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:35,800
the administrative burden is pushed entirely onto the person with the least power to carry it.

25
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:37,380
Which is really inefficient.

26
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:44,960
So an official might assume this once only capability is simply a feature, you know, something a software vendor can just switch on.

27
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:51,340
How do you build this architecture so agencies share data without creating fragmented, centralized databases?

28
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:54,760
It actually requires four distinct layers built correctly.

29
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:56,180
Okay, what's the first layer?

30
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:59,340
First, you need a trusted source for each fact.

31
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:00,840
Meaning what exactly?

32
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:08,560
There must be one authoritative holder rather than, uh, five duplicated copies across different ministries that eventually disagree.

33
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:09,580
Ah, that makes sense.

34
00:02:10,199 --> 00:02:10,780
And the second layer?

35
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:14,300
Second, you must establish a lawful basis for the exchange.

36
00:02:15,020 --> 00:02:18,160
And third, you secure the citizen's consent where appropriate.

37
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:20,380
Right. The consent piece is crucial.

38
00:02:20,820 --> 00:02:21,140
It is.

39
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:27,660
And fourth, you need a real-time method to fetch that fact at the exact moment of service utilizing the data bus.

40
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:31,900
So let us detail that fetch mechanism over the data bus.

41
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:36,520
Um, the bus is not a centralized drive where agencies copy all their records, right?

42
00:02:36,620 --> 00:02:39,700
Because that would be legally problematic and very costly to change.

43
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Yeah, exactly. It is a routing system that retrieves only the requested fact directly from the authoritative source.

44
00:02:47,420 --> 00:02:51,780
So how does this exchange operate in the demonstration country, um, Progressa?

45
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:57,620
Well, in Progressa, consider a learner applying for a credential at the National Examination Authority.

46
00:02:57,880 --> 00:03:06,040
Okay. Under the Once-Only framework, the Examination Authority pre-fills the applicant's identity data directly from the National Identity Authority.

47
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:06,580
Oh, I see.

48
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:10,660
Right. And it then pulls the enrollment data directly from the learner registry.

49
00:03:11,220 --> 00:03:13,780
That specific data is fetched over the bus in seconds.

50
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:18,200
Wow. So the learner is asked for their information exactly once.

51
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:19,080
Exactly once.

52
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:24,440
This really shifts the focus from deploying infrastructure to delivering a policy result.

53
00:03:24,860 --> 00:03:24,960
So

54
00:03:25,860 --> 00:03:29,720
why must once only serve as the North Star for your program's framework?

55
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:32,040
Well, it provides two crucial functions.

56
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:34,420
How so? First, it is strictly measurable.

57
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:42,140
How so? Your program can count how many times a citizen is asked to provide duplicated data and actively drive that number down.

58
00:03:42,660 --> 00:03:44,220
That's a very clear metric to have.

59
00:03:44,620 --> 00:03:47,320
Yeah. And second, it keeps the framework honest.

60
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:48,100
I mean,

61
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:51,700
an impressive bus that nobody uses is just plumbing.

62
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:53,000
Right. Just pipes.

63
00:03:53,340 --> 00:04:01,720
Exactly. It does not become a result until a real service fetches a real fact and a citizen is no longer asked to provide their information twice.

64
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:06,620
Which gives a clear diagnostic picture of whether the reference architecture is functioning for your minister.

65
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:13,280
Definitely. So when explaining interoperability to your minister or cabinet, lead with this outcome.

66
00:04:13,940 --> 00:04:17,339
It is not about data buses. It is a promise to the citizen.

67
00:04:17,980 --> 00:04:22,160
Ask them once, and let the state fetch the rest, lawfully and with consent.

68
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:27,060
Everything else exists entirely to make that one sentence true in your country.

69
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:29,100
Sources are in the video description.
